### === REFRESH TOKEN FLOW ===

### 1. Login — capture the refresh token from the response
### After running this, copy the "refreshToken" value from the response
### into @savedRefreshToken below
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "testuser",
  "password": "TestPassword123!"
}

### Paste the value from step 1's response here before running anything below

### 2. Refresh — happy path. Should return 200 with a NEW access token
### AND a new refresh token. Copy the new refresh token into
### @savedRefreshToken again before running test 3 or 4.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{savedRefreshToken}}"
}

### 3. Reuse detection — run test 2 ONCE (so the token above gets rotated
### and revoked), then immediately run this SAME request again with the
### SAME now-stale @savedRefreshToken value (don't update it first).
### Expect: 401. This is the token showing up a second time after rotation.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{savedRefreshToken}}"
}

### 4. Garbage token — never issued, shouldn't hash-match anything.
### Expect: 401 Invalid refresh token.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "this-was-never-a-real-token-12345"
}

### 5. Empty token — should get caught by [Required] on RefreshRequestDto,
### never even reach the service. Expect: 400 Bad Request.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": ""
}

### === LOGOUT FLOW ===

### 6. Login again fresh, to get a token pair not already burned by the
### tests above. Update @savedRefreshToken from this response before
### continuing to test 7.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Auth/login
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "username": "testuser",
  "password": "TestPassword123!"
}

### 7. Logout — requires a valid ACCESS token in Authorization header
### (this is [Authorize]), body carries the refresh token to revoke.
### Update {{Token}} at the top of the file with the access token from
### test 6 if it's expired. Expect: 204 No Content.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Auth/logout
Authorization: {{Token}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{savedRefreshToken}}"
}

### 8. Try to refresh with the token you just logged out with.
### Expect: 401 — it's now revoked, same as an expired/garbage token
### from the service's point of view.
POST {{HostAddress}}/api/Auth/refresh
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "refreshToken": "{{savedRefreshToken}}"
}